CLAIMS

1. A computer-implemented system for multi-jurisdictional regulatory harmonization comprising:
   a) a multi-jurisdictional regulatory database storing regulations from at least two aviation authorities;
   b) a harmonization engine configured to automatically identify conflicts and overlaps between regulations from different jurisdictions;
   c) a conflict resolution protocol that applies jurisdictional precedence rules to resolve regulatory conflicts;
   d) a unified compliance framework generator that creates consolidated requirements for organizations operating across multiple regulatory domains;
   e) wherein the system automatically updates harmonized requirements when source regulations change.

2. The system of claim 1, wherein the harmonization engine employs semantic analysis to identify functionally equivalent requirements across different regulatory texts.

3. The system of claim 1, wherein the conflict resolution protocol applies configurable precedence weightings based on operational jurisdiction, registration jurisdiction, and bilateral agreements.

4. The system of claim 1, further comprising a real-time synchronization module that monitors regulatory sources for changes and automatically updates harmonized frameworks.

5. The system of claim 1, wherein the unified compliance framework generator optimizes compliance pathways to minimize duplicative requirements across jurisdictions.

6. A method for harmonizing aviation regulatory requirements across multiple jurisdictions comprising:
   a) ingesting regulatory data from multiple aviation authorities into a standardized format;
   b) applying semantic analysis to identify regulatory conflicts and overlaps;
   c) resolving conflicts using jurisdictional precedence rules and bilateral agreements;
   d) generating unified compliance requirements tailored to specific organizational operations;
   e) automatically updating harmonized requirements when source regulations change.

7. The method of claim 6, wherein semantic analysis includes natural language processing to identify functionally equivalent requirements expressed in different regulatory languages.

8. The method of claim 6, wherein conflict resolution includes classification of conflicts into direct contradictions, overlapping standards, and jurisdictional gaps.

9. The method of claim 6, further comprising optimization of compliance pathways to reduce regulatory burden while maintaining full compliance across all applicable jurisdictions.

10. The method of claim 6, wherein unified compliance requirements include implementation sequences, verification procedures, and monitoring protocols customized for specific organizational profiles.

